    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          The decision you are making is not about a drill. It is about a battery interface you will keep buying into for the next ten years, which makes it one of the stickiest purchases in the tool world.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-to-choose" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. How to Choose a Platform
        </h2>

        <p>
          Almost every guide to this question ranks drills. That is the wrong unit of analysis. Every major brand makes a competent drill, and the drill is the first and cheapest thing you will buy. What actually determines whether you are happy in five years is the platform around it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Four things matter, roughly in this order.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Range depth in your categories.</strong> Not total tool count — depth in the specific things you will need. A plumber cares whether the platform has press tools and drain machines. A finish carpenter cares about nailers and track saws. A homeowner cares whether there is a decent hedge trimmer. Total catalogue size is a poor proxy for any of these.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Battery architecture and compatibility.</strong> Does one pack run everything, or does the range split across incompatible systems? Backwards compatibility is worth real money over a decade, and a brand that has broken it before may do so again.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Local availability and service.</strong> The most underrated factor by a wide margin. A platform whose specialty tools are a two-week order, or whose warranty service means shipping a tool across the country, costs you time that does not appear in any review.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Duty cycle match.</strong> Professional-grade durability is measured in hours of sustained load. If you will never reach those hours, you are buying capability that never gets used. If you will exceed them weekly, a value platform will fail in ways that cost more than the saving.
        </p>

        <div id="rankings" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. The Platforms Ranked
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">1. Milwaukee — Best for Trades</h3>

        <p>
          M18 is the most complete professional platform available, with over two hundred and fifty tools and genuine depth in every trade category rather than breadth across easy ones. The REDLINK electronics managing communication between pack and tool are a real engineering advantage under sustained load, and the compact M12 platform is the best small-tool system anyone makes.
        </p>

        <p>
          What sets Milwaukee apart is the trade-specific end of the catalogue: press tools, cable cutters, drain cleaners, pipe threaders. If your work involves any of those, no other platform is competitive. The price is at the top of the market and the tools are heavy, which are the two consistent complaints.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">2. DeWalt — Best All-Round</h3>

        <p>
          The pragmatic default, and the platform hardest to regret. 20V MAX has been fully backwards compatible since 2013, FLEXVOLT extends it cleanly into 60V territory without splitting the ecosystem, and the range covers everything most people need. Availability in North America is unmatched — parts, service and tools on the shelf in almost every town.
        </p>

        <p>
          It also discounts hardest, which makes building a kit out significantly cheaper than on any other professional platform. The tools are heavier and louder than Makita's, and the top end is not quite Milwaukee's in trade specialties, but nothing here is a weakness so much as a trade-off.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">3. Makita — Best Refinement</h3>

        <p>
          LXT is the deepest single-battery professional lineup in existence, and Makita's tools are the ones people describe as feeling engineered. Lighter, quieter, better balanced, with less vibration and batteries that hold capacity better than anyone else's over many cycles. For long working days and finish work it is the platform users are happiest with.
        </p>

        <p>
          Two caveats. The separate 40V XGT platform shares no batteries with LXT, which complicates the decision, and retail availability in North America is thinner than DeWalt's. In many markets outside it, Makita is the professional default and this ranking would flip.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">4. Ryobi — Best for Homeowners</h3>

        <p>
          Nothing else comes close on value. Over three hundred tools on a battery interface unchanged since 1996, at roughly a third of professional prices, with an HP brushless line that closes much of the performance gap for intermittent work. The catalogue includes a great many household tools no professional brand bothers with.
        </p>

        <p>
          Its ceiling is thermal rather than peak power — tools slow and warm under sustained load in a way professional tools do not. For a homeowner that ceiling is never reached. For daily production work it is reached constantly.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">5. Bosch — Best Precision</h3>

        <p>
          Underrated in North America and dominant in parts of Europe. Bosch's measuring and layout tools are the best in the business, its rotary hammers are exceptional, and its 12V line is excellent for cabinet and installation work. The North American cordless range is narrower than the big three, which is the main reason it does not rank higher here.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">6. Metabo HPT — Best Value in a Pro Platform</h3>

        <p>
          Formerly Hitachi, and consistently better than its market share suggests. The MultiVolt system lets a single pack run both 18V and 36V tools, and its nailers and framing tools have a devoted following among carpenters. Prices sit well below the big three for comparable capability. The limitation is range breadth and a thin dealer network.
        </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="by-user" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Which One for Which User
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Electricians, plumbers and HVAC:</strong> Milwaukee, without much argument. The trade-specific tools exist nowhere else, and M12 covers the confined-space work that makes up much of the day.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Framing and general construction:</strong> DeWalt. FLEXVOLT does the heavy cutting and drilling, the tools survive site conditions, and replacements are available same-day almost anywhere.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Finish carpentry and cabinetry:</strong> Makita. Lighter tools held for hours, superb nailers, and track saws built for accuracy rather than throughput.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Landscaping and grounds:</strong> Makita XGT or Milwaukee M18, depending on whether outdoor equipment or general tools dominate. Both have committed properly to cordless outdoor power.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Homeowners and DIY:</strong> Ryobi HP. The value gap is so large that the argument for anything else has to be based on work you are not actually doing.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Serious hobbyists and woodworkers:</strong> Makita or Bosch. Precision, dust extraction and tool feel matter more here than sustained output.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Anyone buying their first ever tool:</strong> almost certainly Ryobi HP, whatever your eventual ambitions. It is cheap enough to be a low-stakes decision, broad enough to cover everything a beginner encounters, and if you later outgrow it the sunk cost is small. Buying a professional platform before you know what work you will actually do is a common and expensive mistake.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">A Word on Voltage Numbers</h3>

        <p>
          It is worth clearing up the marketing here because it distorts a lot of buying decisions. DeWalt's 20V MAX, Makita's 18V LXT and Milwaukee's M18 are all the same thing: five lithium cells in series, nominally eighteen volts, peaking at twenty when fully charged and unloaded. DeWalt quotes the peak, the others quote the nominal. There is no power difference implied by the label.
        </p>

        <p>
          Where voltage genuinely differs is at the top end — DeWalt FLEXVOLT at 60V, Makita XGT at 40V, Metabo HPT MultiVolt at 36V. These are real increases in available power and they exist because some tools, particularly large saws and outdoor equipment, cannot be run properly on an 18V platform. Compare those against each other, not against the 18V-versus-20V labelling.
        </p>

        <h2 id="second-tier" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Brands Worth Knowing
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Festool</strong> occupies its own category. Extraordinary dust extraction, superb track saws and sanders, a systematic approach to storage and workflow, and prices that are difficult to justify unless you are working in occupied homes where dust control is a business requirement. Its users are, almost without exception, extremely happy.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Hart</strong> and <strong>Kobalt</strong> are competent house brands sitting between Ryobi and the professional tier. They are fine tools with narrow ranges, and the risk is platform longevity — a house brand can be discontinued in a way an independent brand cannot.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Harbor Freight's Hercules and Bauer</strong> lines have improved dramatically and represent real value at the bottom of the market. For a tool you need once, they are a reasonable answer. For a platform commitment, the range is too narrow.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Craftsman</strong>, now under the same ownership as DeWalt, sits in the homeowner tier with reasonable tools and a legacy brand name. It is competent without being compelling, and Ryobi generally offers more for similar money.
        </p>

        <h2 id="switching" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Switching Platforms
        </h2>

        <p>
          The honest advice is usually don't. The cost of switching is not the tools you replace — it is the batteries, the chargers, the accessories and the muscle memory, and it compounds with every year you have been on a platform. A ten per cent performance improvement almost never justifies it.
        </p>

        <p>
          There are three situations where switching does make sense. The first is a fundamental change in the work: a homeowner who becomes a contractor, or a general builder who moves into a specialty trade the current platform does not serve. The second is a genuine gap — a tool you need that your platform simply does not make, with no prospect of it appearing. The third is starting fresh anyway after a theft or a long break.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you do switch, do it deliberately and completely rather than gradually. A slow transition means running two battery stocks for years, which is the worst of both worlds.
        </p>

        <h2 id="building" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Building a Kit Sensibly
        </h2>

        <p>
          Start with a two-tool combo kit — drill and impact driver — because you need the charger and batteries regardless and the kit price bundles them efficiently. Then buy bare tools from that point on, adding batteries only when you find yourself waiting on a charge.
        </p>

        <p>
          Mix your battery capacities deliberately. Compact packs for drills and drivers where weight and balance matter, large packs for saws and grinders where runtime does. A kit full of identical mid-size packs is a compromise in both directions.
        </p>

        <p>
          Resist the tool-of-the-month temptation, particularly on value platforms where the low prices make each addition feel trivial. A focused kit of eight tools you use regularly is worth more than twenty that mostly sit in cases, and it costs considerably less.
        </p>

        <p>
          Buy brushless wherever it is offered. The premium has almost disappeared and the advantages — cooler running, longer life, more work per charge — are real on every tool type.
        </p>

        <p>
          And give some thought to storage before the collection outgrows it. Every major platform now sells a modular case system, and committing to one early is far easier than retrofitting it across twenty tools later. It sounds like an afterthought, but the practical difference between a kit you can carry to a job in two trips and one that lives loose in a truck bed is substantial, and it affects how much of the kit you actually use.
        </p>


        <p>
          One last piece of practical advice: keep the receipts and register the tools. Every major brand offers three years or more on cordless tools, and every one of them requires proof of purchase to honour it. A registered tool with a receipt on file is a warranty claim; the same tool without one is a replacement you pay for. It takes two minutes per tool and it is the cheapest insurance in the whole category.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Choosing on the drill review</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Every major brand makes a good drill. The drill tells you almost nothing about whether the platform has the fifteenth tool you will eventually want.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Ignoring what your local store carries</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A platform you cannot buy from locally means every specialty tool is a shipping delay and every warranty claim is a package. Walk the aisles before committing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Mixing platforms to chase deals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Two chargers, two battery stocks, two sets of spares, and half the depth in each. The saving on the discounted tool rarely covers the duplicated batteries.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Buying professional grade for a drawer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Durability you never exercise is money spent on nothing. Match the tier to your actual duty cycle rather than to the tier you would like to be in.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Assuming voltage numbers compare directly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">18V, 20V MAX and 20V are the same nominal platform described differently. Peak versus nominal voltage is a labelling convention, not a performance measure.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cordless tool brand overall?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">For professional trade use, Milwaukee has the deepest and most consistently strong lineup. For general use with the best availability and pricing, DeWalt. For refinement and long working days, Makita. For homeowners, Ryobi ONE+ offers by far the most tool for the money.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Which cordless tool brand is best value?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Ryobi ONE+, comfortably, and it is not close. Over three hundred tools on a battery interface unchanged since 1996, at roughly a third of professional pricing. For anyone not running tools daily it is the rational choice.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Are all 18V batteries the same?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Electrically similar, physically incompatible. Every manufacturer uses a proprietary interface, and most now include electronics that communicate with the tool. Adapters exist but they bypass those protections and void warranties.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a kit or bare tools?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A kit for your first two or three tools, since you need batteries and a charger anyway. Bare tools after that — you will accumulate more packs than you need if every purchase includes them.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Which brand has the most tools?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Ryobi ONE+ has the largest catalogue at over three hundred, though much of that breadth is household items. Milwaukee M18 has the deepest professional trade range, and Makita LXT the deepest single professional battery platform.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth switching brands?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Rarely. The cost of replacing a battery stock and a full tool set almost never justifies a modest performance improvement. Switch only if your work has fundamentally changed or your current platform genuinely lacks a tool you need.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What about brushless versus brushed?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Buy brushless wherever it exists. Brushless motors run cooler, last longer, deliver more power per charge and no longer carry a meaningful price premium. Brushed tools remain only at the very bottom of value ranges.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Which brand is best for a first-time buyer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Ryobi HP if you are maintaining a house, DeWalt if you are learning a trade and want the widest availability. Both are easy to enter and neither locks you out of anything you are likely to need in the first few years.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

